          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would antibiotics help?",
          "answer": "Antibiotics work against bacterial infections. They do not work against viral infections. The sources I searched describe the general pattern, but they do not give a full list of which infections are bacterial.\n\n**Viral illnesses (antibiotics do not help):** The retrieved guidance says that common colds, most sore throats, and acute bronchitis are viral in most cases. Antibiotics do not shorten these illnesses. They also cause side effects and can encourage antibiotic-resistant bacteria. (Source: *Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship*.) Influenza is also viral. The flu guidance says antibiotics have no effect on influenza. They are used only for bacterial complications. (Source: *Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment*.)\n\n**Bacterial infections (antibiotics may help):** The retrieved passages do not describe a full list of bacterial infections. They do mention uncomplicated urinary tract infection, where antibiotics are part of standard treatment. Guidance differs on how quickly to start them. (Source: *Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence*.)\n\nWhether an antibiotic would help depends on the specific infection. Please tell me which condition or symptoms you mean, and I can give a more specific answer. A doctor or pharmacist can also judge whether an infection is likely to be bacterial.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\n[sources: guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment]",
