          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When would they actually be needed?",
          "answer": "I am not sure what \"they\" refers to. Could you tell me which treatment you mean, and the condition or situation it relates to? For example, antibiotics for your cough, or for a urinary infection?\n\nFrom the material I searched, here is what the guidance says about when antibiotics are needed. The sources do not cover every case.\n\n**For a cold or cough after a cold:** Antibiotics are generally not needed. The guidance says the common cold, most sore throats, and acute bronchitis are viral in most cases. Antibiotics do not shorten them, and their harms are described as outweighing the benefit. (Source: *Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated*.)\n\n**For a urinary tract infection:** The retrieved guidance describes antibiotics as part of standard treatment for uncomplicated infection, meaning infection in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract. Other guidance says some mild cases can be managed first without antibiotics, as a shared decision. (Source: *Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence*.)\n\n**For a possible secondary bacterial pneumonia:** The influenza guidance says this classically appears as a worsening after several days of improvement. The sources I searched do not say which antibiotic or when to start one. A clinician would decide that. (Source: *Influenza*.)\n\nIf you tell me which of these applies to you, I can go into more detail. A doctor or pharmacist can judge whether antibiotics are needed in your case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\n[sources: guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment, condition-influenza, condition-upper-respiratory-infection]",
